WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.982
Picture the counter of a post office.

2
00:00:04.982 --> 00:00:09.668
A customer walks up with a parcel and asks: how much to send this to Lisbon?

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.807
The post office doesn't carry parcels that far.

4
00:00:12.807 --> 00:00:21.220
So it asks three courier companies, Northline, Eastgate and Fjord, and tells the customer the cheapest price within a third of a second.

5
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:27.339
A program that does this is a quote service: it gives a price for a job before anyone books it.

6
00:00:27.339 --> 00:00:29.639
Over three sittings, we'll build one.

7
00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:37.308
A client, the program that asks, opens a connection to our server and sends one line: 7, Lisbon.

8
00:00:37.308 --> 00:00:39.625
The server answers with one line.

9
00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:40.913
Read it aloud.

10
00:00:40.913 --> 00:00:42.455
Request 7 went fine.

11
00:00:42.455 --> 00:00:48.237
Sending the parcel to Lisbon costs 12.42, and Northline offered that price.

12
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:54.951
The 7 is the client's own number for its question, a request id, and the server repeats it on the answer.

13
00:00:54.951 --> 00:00:57.731
It works like the stub you get for your coat.

14
00:00:57.731 --> 00:01:00.263
Show the number, get the right coat back.

15
00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:04.548
The three sittings build the program from the outside in.

16
00:01:04.548 --> 00:01:09.293
This sitting builds the counter, on Netty, the Java library for network servers.

17
00:01:09.293 --> 00:01:12.319
The next one makes the phone calls to the couriers.

18
00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:15.700
The last keeps the service standing when a crowd arrives.

19
00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:19.284
Now, a question that's easy to be shy about.

20
00:01:19.284 --> 00:01:23.803
The clerk at the counter needs a price, and the price is a phone call away.

21
00:01:23.803 --> 00:01:26.635
A courier answers in about a tenth of a second.

22
00:01:26.635 --> 00:01:29.225
So why not just pick up the phone and wait?

23
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.696
Look closer at the clerk.

24
00:01:33.696 --> 00:01:36.058
He owns a row of mailboxes, for good.

25
00:01:36.058 --> 00:01:40.717
Letters from many customers land in that row, and he serves them in turn.

26
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:41.913
Let's map that.

27
00:01:41.913 --> 00:01:46.876
The clerk is an event loop: one thread that serves many connections in turn.

28
00:01:46.876 --> 00:01:51.185
Each mailbox is a channel, Netty's object for one open connection.

29
00:01:51.185 --> 00:01:54.059
And the phone call is our call to a courier.

30
00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:59.784
Ten customers send one question each, at the same moment, all into this clerk's row.

31
00:01:59.784 --> 00:02:05.518
Each price takes the courier a tenth of a second, and the clerk waits on the phone for each one.

32
00:02:05.518 --> 00:02:07.907
When does the last customer get a reply?

33
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:16.225
The clerk picks up the phone for the first letter and holds it until the price comes back.

34
00:02:16.225 --> 00:02:18.651
Only then does he open the second letter.

35
00:02:18.651 --> 00:02:19.539
Then the third.

36
00:02:19.533 --> 00:02:23.103
Ten customers, ten phone calls, one after another.

37
00:02:23.103 --> 00:02:25.387
About a second for the last one.

38
00:02:25.387 --> 00:02:33.740
One run of the lesson's demo measured 1,163 milliseconds, and the courier never saw more than one question at a time.

39
00:02:34.467 --> 00:02:35.551
Now the other way.

40
00:02:35.551 --> 00:02:41.513
The clerk asks for the price, takes a ticket for the answer, and goes straight back to his letters.

41
00:02:41.513 --> 00:02:46.753
When a price arrives, it lands in his in-tray, and he writes the reply between letters.

42
00:02:46.967 --> 00:02:49.629
All ten questions are out at once.

43
00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:53.700
The same demo answered everyone in 146 milliseconds.

44
00:02:54.633 --> 00:02:55.988
And here's the trap.

45
00:02:55.988 --> 00:03:03.372
The first version passes every test with one customer, which is exactly how many customers a simple test has.

46
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.012
So the point is this.

47
00:03:05.012 --> 00:03:10.056
Once customers share a clerk, one customer's wait becomes everybody's wait.

48
00:03:10.967 --> 00:03:14.410
That gives the one rule every part of this service obeys.

49
00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.041
The event loop never waits.

50
00:03:16.041 --> 00:03:20.693
Hand the ticket back, and finish the job on the loop when the answer arrives.

51
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.097
Here our picture breaks.

52
00:03:23.097 --> 00:03:27.339
A real post office would notice the queue and open a second counter.

53
00:03:27.339 --> 00:03:29.522
Netty never adds a loop on its own.

54
00:03:29.522 --> 00:03:32.891
It doesn't even notice that the clerk is on the phone.

55
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:39.776
Add a second clerk yourself, and each one serves five customers, still on the phone: about half a second.

56
00:03:39.776 --> 00:03:41.683
More clerks divide the damage.

57
00:03:41.683 --> 00:03:43.019
They don't remove it.

58
00:03:43.933 --> 00:03:46.399
Two more details are there on purpose.

59
00:03:46.399 --> 00:03:49.643
First, the server reads at most 64 bytes per line.

60
00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:57.554
Without a limit, a client that never ends its line would make the server keep every byte, waiting for an end that never comes.

61
00:03:57.554 --> 00:04:00.909
A longer line gets an error, and the connection closes.

62
00:04:01.633 --> 00:04:03.391
Second, the request id.

63
00:04:03.391 --> 00:04:07.822
One client sends two questions: 7, Lisbon, then 8, Dublin.

64
00:04:07.822 --> 00:04:10.267
Dublin's price comes back first.

65
00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:12.177
Which reply leaves first?

66
00:04:14.967 --> 00:04:15.589
Dublin's.

67
00:04:15.589 --> 00:04:20.978
A reply leaves when its price arrives, so a quick answer overtakes a slow one.

68
00:04:20.978 --> 00:04:22.015
Then 7, Lisbon.

69
00:04:22.015 --> 00:04:26.023
The client matches them by their numbers, like coat stubs.

70
00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:30.305
Here's the counter as a line of stations, a pipeline.

71
00:04:30.305 --> 00:04:35.267
Each station is a handler, a small object that reacts to the channel's events.

72
00:04:35.467 --> 00:04:39.518
The frame station cuts the bytes into lines of at most 64 bytes.

73
00:04:39.518 --> 00:04:44.267
The decoder turns a line into text, and replies go out through the encoder.

74
00:04:44.267 --> 00:04:46.736
Flow control waits for its own section.

75
00:04:46.736 --> 00:04:52.560
The last station, QuoteHandler, answers the question, and that's where the phone call hides.

76
00:04:53.467 --> 00:04:57.138
In code, the clerk on the phone is one innocent word: join.

77
00:04:57.138 --> 00:04:59.813
It holds the thread until the answer comes.

78
00:04:59.813 --> 00:05:05.413
The fix asks for the price and says what to do when it arrives, on the channel's own loop.

79
00:05:05.413 --> 00:05:06.720
Kotlin says the same.

80
00:05:07.633 --> 00:05:14.261
So look in your own code for join, get, or runBlocking inside anything that runs on an event loop.

81
00:05:14.261 --> 00:05:16.628
Each one is the clerk on the phone.

82
00:05:17.533 --> 00:05:20.088
So, why not just wait a tenth of a second?

83
00:05:20.088 --> 00:05:22.704
Because that tenth of a second isn't yours.

84
00:05:22.704 --> 00:05:25.259
It belongs to everyone in the clerk's row.

85
00:05:25.259 --> 00:05:31.341
The counter hands each question back as a ticket, numbers its replies, and never picks up the phone.

86
00:05:31.567 --> 00:05:32.427
Quick check.

87
00:05:32.427 --> 00:05:35.294
One client sends 9, Oslo, then 10, Rome.

88
00:05:35.294 --> 00:05:37.230
Rome's price arrives first.

89
00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:41.961
Which reply leaves first, and how does the client tell them apart?

90
00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:46.971
The reply for 10 leaves first.

91
00:05:46.971 --> 00:05:52.485
The client reads the number at the front of each reply and matches it to its own question.
